To add a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator, you can first define the size of the Gaussian kernel (e.g., 3x3 or 5x5) and then apply the `fspecial` function to generate a Gaussian filter. Here's the modified code:

```matlab
% Read input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Define size of Gaussian kernel
gaussian_kernel_size = 5;
% Generate Gaussian filter
gaussian_filter = fspecial('gaussian', [gaussian_kernel_size gaussian_kernel_size], 0.5);
% Apply Gaussian filter to input image
blurred_image = filter2(gaussian_filter, gray_image, 'replicate');
% Define Sobel operators in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator to blurred image
edge_image_x = conv2(double(blurred_image), sobel_x, 'same');
edge_image_y = conv2(double(blurred_image), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions and display result
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
imshow(edge_image);
```
In this modified code, we added a new variable called `gaussian_kernel_size`, which specifies the size of the Gaussian kernel. We also generated a Gaussian filter using the `fspecial` function and applied it to the input image using the `filter2` function. Finally, we applied the Sobel operators to the blurred image instead of the original one. This helps to reduce noise in the image before edge detection.

